  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?",
  "worked_solution": "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}(2-3\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(2-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(2-3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}.$",
